[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by tenant and by what each report says, the
reports in it come from three different problems. Each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. The other two problems stay open after that fix ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
date with both parts at 12 or below comes out with day and month swapped: a 7 April booking
lands on 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names. It covers every report from a day-first tenant
that did not come in through a form showing a month-first hint.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The tenant's people type what the hint
says, month first, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The date flips
before the parser sees it, so the parser fix does not touch it. It covers reports from tenants
on booking-v3 that came in through the form. Intake tags these by the reporter's browser, so
the `hint` tag and this problem disagree; the tenant decides, as in `causes.ts`.

### `one-day`: the date moves one day, not a month

These reports do not describe a swap. The booking moves back by one day: Tuesday becomes
Monday, a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and a client in Toronto gets Monday, an invite arrives
before the booking was made, every June booking is one day off. A day/month swap cannot do
that. The desk already marked them in `desk-notes.json` and counts them under `parser`, where
they are filed by tenant; the table keeps that count. They are a separate problem with a
separate fix, most likely in how a date is converted between time zones.

## What changes on the desk

- Matching on subject line put three problems in one ticket. When reports in one ticket
  describe different changes to a date, split it, even if the subjects match.
- Count a problem by the tenant and what the report says, not by the intake tag.
- The export repeats some reports across pages. Count each id once.

